---
import BaseLayout from '../../components/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { TOOLS } from '../../lib/tools';
import { collectionPageGraph } from '../../lib/schema';
import { siteConfig } from '../../config/site';

const tools = TOOLS.filter((t) => ['yarn', 'knitting', 'crochet'].includes(t.category));
const title = 'Knitting & Crochet Calculators';
const description =
  'Free knitting and crochet calculators for yarn yardage, gauge, cast-on stitches, even increases, and crochet blanket starting chains.';
const jsonLd = collectionPageGraph({
  name: title,
  description,
  path: '/knitting-crochet/',
  crumbs: [
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Knit & crochet', path: '/knitting-crochet/' },
  ],
});
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/knitting-crochet/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="container">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Knit & crochet' }]} />
    <h1>Knitting &amp; crochet calculators</h1>
    <p class="takeaway">
      <strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Start with yarn weight and gauge, then estimate yardage or stitch counts—every tool
      here stays on-device. Example: a 50×60″ worsted knit throw at FiberCalc’s density with 10% waste is about 1,815 yd
      (10 skeins at 200 yd each).
    </p>
    <p class="meta-row"><span>Last reviewed {siteConfig.lastReviewed}</span></p>
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What these tools solve</h2>
      <p>
        Use yarn estimators when you are shopping for a <a href="/tools/yarn-blanket-calculator/">blanket</a>,
        <a href="/tools/yarn-sweater-calculator/">sweater</a>, <a href="/tools/yarn-hat-calculator/">hat</a>,
        <a href="/tools/yarn-scarf-calculator/">scarf</a>, or
        <a href="/tools/yarn-amigurumi-calculator/">amigurumi</a>. Switch to the
        <a href="/tools/knitting-gauge-calculator/">knitting gauge calculator</a> when you need stitches per inch or must
        resize a pattern. For stitch counts—not yarn—use the
        <a href="/tools/crochet-blanket-calculator/">crochet blanket starting chain calculator</a>, the
        <a href="/tools/cast-on-calculator/">cast-on calculator</a>, and the
        <a href="/tools/increase-decrease-calculator/">even increase/decrease calculator</a>. The
        <a href="/tools/yarn-substitution-calculator/">yarn substitution calculator</a> converts yards between CYC
        weight categories using FiberCalc planning midpoints.
      </p>

      <h2>How to measure</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Swatch in the stitch pattern you will use; wash and block the way you will finish the project.</li>
        <li>Measure stitches and rows over 4″ when possible—small swatches magnify counting error.</li>
        <li>Read the CYC category icon on the ball band; brand names like “DK” vs “worsted” can disagree.</li>
        <li>Note yards (or meters) and grams per skein so skein counts stay honest when labels differ.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Worked example: throw yardage</h2>
      <p>
        Area 50 × 60 = 3,000 in². FiberCalc worsted (#4) knit density is 0.55 yd/in² → 1,650 yd before waste; ×1.10 =
        <strong>1,815 yd</strong>. At 200 yd/skein that is ceil(1,815 ÷ 200) = <strong>10 skeins</strong>. Crochet uses
        the same density ×1.28 ≈ 2,323 yd for the same footprint. These density factors are site heuristics—documented on
        <a href="/methodology/#yarn-density">Methodology</a>—not a Craft Yarn Council publication. Prefer a pattern’s
        tested yardage when you have one.
      </p>

      <h2>Worked example: cast-on from gauge</h2>
      <p>
        If your swatch is 18 stitches over 4″ (4.5 sts/in) and you want a 20″ hat circumference with a little negative
        ease, 20 × 4.5 ≈ 90 stitches before you adjust for pattern multiples. The cast-on calculator applies gauge and
        ease so you are not doing that multiplication on scrap paper mid-cast-on.
      </p>

      <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Estimating yardage before locking gauge—looser fabric usually burns more yarn per square inch.</li>
        <li>Mixing dye lots because the estimate was “almost” enough; buy an extra skein when lots are limited.</li>
        <li>Substituting by name only instead of matching CYC category, gauge, and fiber behavior.</li>
        <li>Using crochet and knit yardage interchangeably; crochet typically needs more yarn for the same area.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Guides and reference</h2>
      <p>
        Guides such as <a href="/guides/how-much-yarn-for-a-blanket/">how much yarn for a blanket</a>,
        <a href="/guides/how-much-yarn-for-a-sweater/">sweater yarn</a>,
        <a href="/guides/how-many-skeins-for-a-scarf/">scarf skeins</a>,
        <a href="/guides/does-crochet-use-more-yarn-than-knitting/">crochet vs knit yarn</a>,
        <a href="/guides/knitting-gauge-explained/">knitting gauge explained</a>, and
        <a href="/guides/how-to-increase-evenly-across-a-row/">even increases</a> explain the “why”; the tools do the
        arithmetic. See also <a href="/reference/yarn-weights/">CYC yarn weight standards</a> and the
        <a href="/tools/needle-hook-converter/">needle &amp; hook converter</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="tool-grid">
      {tools.map((t) => (
        <a class="tool-link" href={t.path}><h2>{t.title}</h2><p>{t.description}</p></a>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
